course: dictionary
lesson: 12537
title: Details
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A detail is a small fact or part that helps make up something bigger. Details help you picture what is happening in a story. «The old boat had cracked red paint and a rope tied to a rusty post.» The cracked paint, the red colour and the rusty post are details that make the boat real.
explain: Details also give exact information. A form may ask for your contact details: your name, address and phone number. These are the facts someone needs to reach you, so they must be correct.
explain: In maths, not every detail is needed. Read the question carefully and pick the details that matter. «Tom has 4 pens and a blue bag. How many pens does he have?» The number 4 is the detail you need; the blue bag is extra.
explain: Some phrases use this word. To go in detail means to explain every part fully. To pay attention to detail means to notice the small things that others miss.
fact: A detail is a small fact or part of something bigger.
fact: «Details» is the plural: more than one detail.
fact: Details in a picture are the small, careful parts, like the lines on a leaf.
fact: Your contact details are your name, address and phone number.
fact: To detail something (verb) means to describe it fully, part by part.
exercise: «The artist painted every vein on the leaf, adding fine details.» What are the fine details? | *Small, careful parts of the picture | Large, empty spaces | Loud noises | Bright colours only
exercise: Mia must write her contact details on a form. Which details should she write? | Her best friend's dog's name | Her favourite colour and song | *Her name, address and phone number | The weather that day
exercise: «Tom has 4 pens and a blue bag. How many pens does he have?» Which detail do you need to answer? | *The number 4 | Pens and bag together | Tom | A blue bag
test: What does the verb «detail» mean? | To count money | To hide something from everyone | To run very fast | *To describe something fully, part by part
test: «Her story was full of details about the forest, like the smell of wet moss.» What do the details do? | Show the name of the author's school | *Help the reader picture the forest | Give a map of the country | List only numbers
exam: Why should you keep your personal details private online? | Because they are always wrong | *So strangers cannot use your address, phone number or passwords | Because they change every day | Because they are too long to type
